---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_218",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 218,
  "text": "[[859.1]] <b>180.  Plura similiter posita simul aequivalent uni [März bis April 1687 (?)]</b>\n [[859.2]] <tab/>[März bis April 1687 (?)] </p>\n<p> [[859.8]] In isto calculo nihil aliud adhibetur, quam pro inexistentibus quidem, ut plura\n[[859.9]] similiter posita simul aequivaleant uni. Ut <i>A</i> ^&.Sg <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> ubi <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> eodem modo se habent,\n[[859.10]] et pro ambobus sic scriptis poni potest unum. Unde huc redit <i>et</i> si poneremus <i>et AB</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>,\n[[859.11]] nam signum ^&.Sg <Leibniz special character> mihi non significat additionem, vel aliquid aliud sed simpliciter desi-\n[[859.12]] gnationem. Hinc et ista applicari possunt ad totum distributivum. Si distributivo addas\n[[859.13]] comparationem oritur <Fn><i>Darunter</i>: Opt</Fn> universale, si collectivi positionem fieri potest continuum. </p>\n<p> [[859.14]] Si duo Termini ponantur affecti diversis signis, et ponendo eos coincidere, se mutuo\n[[859.15]] tollant, id signum dicitur ^&.sv <Leibniz special character>. Verbi gratia ^&.Sg <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> ^&.sv <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i>, si ponamus <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i>, seu ^&.Sg <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> ^&.sv <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> et id\n[[859.16]] aequivalet Nihilo quasi nihil plane positum fuisset. Et ita patet quod signum detractionis\n[[859.17]] seu contrarietatis nihil aliud est quam expectativa futurae sublationis, ut <i>L</i> ─ <i>M</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>P</i> si\n[[859.18]] nihil commune ipsis <i>L</i> et <i>M</i> hoc significat <i>P</i> + <i>M</i> fore ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>. </p>\n<p> [[859.19]] Cognoscere utrum duo habeant aliquid commune, quod insit utrique, et invenire\n[[859.20]] quodnam illud sit. Sint <i>A</i> et <i>B</i>, quaeritur an sit aliquod <i>M</i>, quod insit utrique. Solutio: fiat\n[[859.21]] ex duobus unum <i>A</i> ^&.Sg <Leibniz special character> <i>B</i> quod sit <i>L</i> <i>per post. 1</i> et ab <i>L</i> auferatur unum constituentium <i>A</i>\n[[859.22]] <i>postul. 2</i> residuum sit <i>N</i>, tunc si <i>N</i> coincidit alteri coincidentium <i>B</i>, nihil habebunt\n[[859.23]] commune. Si non coincidant, habebunt aliquid commune, quod invenietur, si residuum <i>N</i>\n[[859.24]] quod necessario inest ipsi <i>B</i> detrahatur a <i>B</i> <i>per post. 2</i> et restabit <i>M</i> quaesitum commune\n[[859.25]] ipsis <i>A</i> et <i>B</i>. Q. E. F. </p>\n<p> [[860.1]] Si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> et <i>A</i> + <i>N</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>, <i>B</i> et <i>N</i> habent aliquid commune. Si <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> erit <i>B</i> in\n[[860.2]] <i>A</i>. Residuum nihil habet commune cum detracto, reductis omnibus ad incommunicantia\n[[860.3]] <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>A</i> + <i>N</i>, <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>G</i> + <i>M</i>, [<i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>H</i> + <i>M</i>], fiat <i>G</i> + <i>M</i> + <i>H</i> + <i>M</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>G</i> + <i>M</i> + <i>N</i>. Ergo\n[[860.4]] <i>N</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>H</i>. </p>\n<p> [[860.5]] Si quid plurimis positis aut remotis coincidere intelligatur, ista dicuntur <b>constituentia</b>,\n[[860.6]] hoc <b>constitutum</b>. Hinc omnia inexistentia sunt constituentia, non contra.\n[[860.7]] Etiam quidvis per quodlibet constitui potest. Ut sit <i>N</i> constituendum per <i>A</i>, fiat (per\n[[860.8]] post. 1) <i>A</i> + <i>N</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i>, unde si <i>N</i> et <i>A</i> sint incommunicantia fiet <i>L</i> ─ <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>N</i>. Sin communicantia\n[[860.9]] sint duo ut <i>A</i> et <i>B</i> et <i>B</i> constitui debeat per <i>A</i>, fiat rursus <i>A</i> + <i>B</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> et posito ipsis <i>A</i>\n[[860.10]] et <i>B</i> commune esse <i>N</i>, fiet <i>A</i> ^&.SE <Leibniz special character> <i>L</i> ─ <i>B</i> + <i>N</i>. Hoc problema praesupponit aliud problema de\n[[860.11]] inveniendis communibus existentibus. </p>\n",
  "number": "180.",
  "title": "Plura similiter posita simul aequivalent uni",
  "date": "[März bis April 1687 (?)]"
};
---

